=> Azure Queue Messaging Services

    -> Azure supports two queue mechanisms
        - Azure Service Bus Queues
        - Azure Storage Queues

    -> Purpose of Queues
        - Used for asynchronous communication between applications/services
        - Helps in decoupling producer and consumer systems
        - Improves scalability and reliability
        - Useful when one service is slow, busy, or temporarily unavailable

    -> Basic Queue Flow
        - Producer sends message to queue
        - Queue stores message temporarily
        - Consumer processes message later

        Example:
            Producer ---> Queue ---> Consumer

    -> Azure Service Bus Queues
        - Part of Azure Messaging Infrastructure
        - Supports:
            - Queuing
            - Publish/Subscribe
            - Advanced integration patterns
        - Designed for enterprise-level messaging
        - Can integrate applications across:
            - Different communication protocols
            - Different trust domains
            - Different network environments
            - Different data contracts

    -> Features of Service Bus Queues
        - Supports advanced messaging patterns
        - FIFO support
        - Dead-letter queues
        - Transactions support
        - Duplicate detection
        - Sessions support
        - Reliable message delivery

    -> Use Cases of Service Bus Queues
        - Microservices communication
        - Enterprise application integration
        - Financial transactions
        - Order processing systems
        - Event-driven architectures

    -> Azure Storage Queues
        - Part of Azure Storage Infrastructure
        - Designed for simple and highly scalable messaging
        - Stores large numbers of messages
        - Accessible globally using:
            - HTTP
            - HTTPS

    -> Features of Storage Queues
        - Very high scalability
        - Cost-effective
        - Can contain millions of messages
        - Simple queue implementation
        - Suitable for background processing

    -> Common Use Cases of Storage Queues
        - Background jobs
        - Async task processing
        - Workload buffering
        - Logging systems
        - Image/video processing pipelines

    -> Key Difference Between Both

        Service Bus Queue
            - Advanced enterprise messaging
            - Rich messaging features
            - More reliable and intelligent routing
            - Higher cost

        Storage Queue
            - Simple message storage
            - Massive scalability
            - Cheaper solution
            - Limited advanced features

