=> Choose a Message Queue Solution

    -> Azure provides two queue solutions
        - Service Bus Queues
        - Storage Queues

    -> Selection depends on
        - Application requirements
        - Message size
        - Ordering guarantees
        - Reliability needs
        - Transaction support
        - Scalability requirements

    -> Consider Using Service Bus Queues

        -> No polling requirement
            - Service Bus supports long polling
            - Messages can be received without continuously checking the queue
            - Uses TCP-based protocols

        -> FIFO Message Ordering
            - Supports guaranteed First-In-First-Out delivery
            - Messages are processed in exact sending order

            Example:
                Message1 -> Message2 -> Message3
                Consumer receives in same order

        -> Duplicate Detection
            - Automatically detects duplicate messages
            - Prevents processing same message multiple times

        -> Parallel Long-Running Streams
            - Messages can be grouped using Session ID
            - Each consumer processes one stream/session

            Example:
                Session A -> Consumer 1
                Session B -> Consumer 2

            - Useful for ordered workflow processing

        -> Transaction Support
            - Supports transactional behavior
            - Multiple operations can succeed/fail together atomically

            Example:
                - Send message
                - Update database
                - Complete together

        -> Large Message Support
            - Suitable for messages larger than 64 KB
            - Supports:
                - Up to 256 KB
                - Up to 1 MB depending on tier
                - Up to 100 MB in advanced scenarios

        -> Role-Based Access Control
            - Supports different permissions for:
                - Senders
                - Receivers
            - Improves security management

    -> Common Use Cases of Service Bus Queues
        - Enterprise applications
        - Banking systems
        - Order processing
        - Financial transactions
        - Workflow orchestration
        - Microservices communication

    -> Consider Using Storage Queues

        -> Very Large Queue Storage
            - Suitable when queue stores more than 80 GB of messages
            - Supports massive scalability

        -> Message Processing Progress Tracking
            - Workers can track processing state
            - Helpful during worker crashes/failures

            Example:
                - Worker processes 50%
                - Worker crashes
                - Another worker resumes from saved progress

        -> Transaction Logging
            - Supports server-side logs
            - Tracks all queue transactions
            - Useful for monitoring and auditing

    -> Common Use Cases of Storage Queues
        - Background jobs
        - Batch processing
        - Logging systems
        - Video/Image processing
        - Large-scale async processing

    -> Quick Decision Guide

        Use Service Bus Queue When:
            - FIFO ordering is required
            - Transactions are needed
            - Duplicate detection is needed
            - Enterprise messaging is required
            - Advanced features are needed

        Use Storage Queue When:
            - Massive storage is required
            - Simple queueing is enough
            - Very high scalability is needed
            - Cost-effective solution is preferred

